{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The reliability gain is genuine if and only if perturbing the checklist's decision-relevant features while preserving its formal structure degrades panel accuracy less than it degrades a hypothetical single expert forced to use the same perturbed rule; mere compliance is distinguished by showing panel accuracy collapses synchronously with checklist-feature alignment, proving the panel adds no independent signal beyond checklist execution.","counterconditions":["The evaluation does not include a perturbation or degradation component, making it impossible to distinguish orthogonal signal from compliance.","The panel relies on un-encodable tacit knowledge that the checklist does not capture but that nonetheless tracks the right answer, meaning the checklist is epiphenomenal rather than the panel.","The checklist is perfectly complete and lossless relative to the domain, in which case there is no 'mere compliance' to distinguish from genuine understanding."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist is a lossy linear projection of the full feature space: it cannot distinguish genuine understanding from correlated compliance. Genuine reliability gain requires the panel to contribute a residual signal orthogonal to the checklist itself. The within-evaluation test forces this separation by perturbing checklist features: if the panel's accuracy degrades identically to a rule-bound automaton, the panel is epiphenomenal to the checklist (mere compliance). If the panel maintains accuracy via un-encoded features, the gain is genuine. This follows the covering principle that reliability is a property of the combined system, not the checklist artifact alone, and any valid test must isolate the system's generative contribution from its procedural mimicry.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3